4.4  cgroups

TL;DR
  - A cgroup is a group of processes that the kernel measures and limits
    together: CPU, memory, disk I/O, number of processes.
  - systemd puts every service and every login session in a cgroup of its own,
    such as system.slice/nginx.service.
  - Containers are built on them: a container's memory limit is a cgroup limit.

      systemd-cgls                                   # the tree
      systemd-cgtop                                  # usage per cgroup, live
      sudo systemctl set-property nginx.service MemoryMax=1G

On your machine
  Version   cgroup v2
  Yours     /user.slice/user-1000.slice/session-2.scope
  Controls  cpu, memory, pids
  Memory    limited to 8.0 GiB (set on /user.slice/user-1000.slice)

Pitfalls
  - A process that reaches its cgroup's memory limit is OOM-killed, even when
    the machine has free RAM (see 5.2).
  - systemctl set-property is permanent: add --runtime for a change that ends at
    the next reboot.
  - Killing a service's main process can leave its children running;
    systemctl stop stops the whole cgroup.
  - Most current distros use cgroup v2 only; some old container tools still
    expect v1.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 4.4 --why

Related
  Commands  systemd-cgls, systemd-cgtop, systemctl
  Paths     /sys/fs/cgroup, /proc/self/cgroup
  Hub       idk processes Processes & scheduling
  See also  idk 5.2 The OOM killer
  See also  idk 11.1 Units and timers
